[{:db/id 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" "question-9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" "question-60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" "question-4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" :question/id #uuid "1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162229" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608" :answer-field/id #uuid "e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" "answer-617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" "answer-2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" "answer-11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" "answer-8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" :answer/id #uuid "e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" :answer/id #uuid "617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" :answer/id #uuid "11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d" :answer/id #uuid "8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5" :question/id #uuid "9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162228" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=z\\,j-y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,j-y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\pi [t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12" :answer-field/id #uuid "42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" "answer-58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" "answer-d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" "answer-8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" "answer-adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" :answer/id #uuid "58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" :answer/id #uuid "d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" :answer/id #uuid "8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d" :answer/id #uuid "adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/id #uuid "54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162999" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,z={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+3y\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =(-3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+\\frac{13}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3+\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/id #uuid "e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" "answer-b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" "answer-9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" "answer-9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" "answer-0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/id #uuid "f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/id #uuid "9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/id #uuid "9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/id #uuid "0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/id #uuid "60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162314" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=t+{t}^{2},\\,z=-t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (1+2t)+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\cdot (-1-2t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}+{t}^{4}+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{2}{5}+1-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/id #uuid "db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" "answer-3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" "answer-e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" "answer-b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" "answer-a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/id #uuid "ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/id #uuid "3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/id #uuid "e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/id #uuid "a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/id #uuid "9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162364" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" "answer-1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" "answer-3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" "answer-aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" "answer-83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/id #uuid "da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/id #uuid "3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/id #uuid "aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/id #uuid "83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/id #uuid "4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/math-academy-id "q-162373" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/id #uuid "257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" "answer-20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" "answer-06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" "answer-c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" "answer-fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/id #uuid "54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/id #uuid "06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/id #uuid "c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/id #uuid "fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135163 :tutorial/content "We're now ready to state the main result of the lesson: \n\n*Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as **Stokes' theorem** and can be considered a generalization of Green's theorem. Let's spend some time building some intuition behind this result.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field in $\\mathbb R^3.$ Stokes' theorem states that the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf F$ on the surface $S$ equals the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary $C$ of $S.$ \n\n![](images/30/308aec6213f8dd42c4f853078c0305901bc19568bfd5132221bd72743f4aa58a.png)\n\nConsider the left-hand side of the above picture, and suppose we have a point $P$ that lies on $S.$ \n\nThe magnitude of the flux of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ through $S$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n.\n$$\n\nWe can think of this quantity as the amount of \"swirl\" of $\\mathbf F$ at $P$ that acts tangentially to $S$ at $P.$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the flux of the curl through $S$ at every point over the entire surface $S,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf n\\, \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhich we usually write as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us the left-hand side of Stokes' theorem.\n\nLet's now look at the right-hand side of our image. The macroscopic curl is simply the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ along the boundary of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the microscopic curls with the circulation, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nStokes' theorem can be summarized in a single picture as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34b39be1166c0556eaad00a473684916c0725c186829f6085db1a322da29612a.png)\n\nWe can immediately deduce a remarkable fact. Notice the value of the surface integral depends only on the circulation at the boundary. Therefore, within a given vector field, if two surfaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ have the same boundary $C,$ then their respective surface integrals will be equal!\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S_1} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_{S_2} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135162 :tutorial/content "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134013 :activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
